  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule exist, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-39a54ee0 Web logs overview\"** (`eff-39a54ee0-web-logs-overview-ff8f01a86f02`) has six panels on the `Kibana Sample Data Logs` data view:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Average response bytes (average of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n- Requests by response code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`)\n- Top requesting countries (bar, terms on `geo.dest`)\n\nI set the dashboard's time range to the last 5 years rather than the default 7 days. I chose this because I couldn't confirm when the sample data was loaded, and a narrower window might show empty panels. Narrow it if you want.\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-39a54ee0 5xx spike\"** (`47b7e793-b460-4381-847e-e4a328672c70`) is an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- Runs every 1m, looks back over a 5m window on `timestamp`.\n- Query matches `response.keyword` values starting with `5`.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Status is enabled; its current status is `pending` until the first run.\n\n**Things to know:**\n- `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` is empty in this session, so I couldn't run raw Elasticsearch queries to profile the values. I used the Kibana MCP tools for everything, which work. The field list came from `describe_data_view`, but I did not check the actual distribution of response codes. The 5xx match is by prefix on the string field, not a numeric range, so it assumes `response` holds standard three-digit HTTP codes.\n- I did not verify that the rule fires. Its first run will show whether the sample data has any 5xx spikes in the window.",
